{"name":"Fading Fog","hex":"#e8e4e0","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fading","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of fade."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process by which something fades; gradual diminishment.","An Irish dance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fading"},{"term":"fog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick cloud that forms near the ground; the obscurity of such a cloud.","A mist or film clouding a surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with or as if with fog.","To become obscured in condensation or water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fading","blocks":[{"text":"In wireless communications, fading is the variation of signal attenuation over variables like time, geographical position, and radio frequency. Fading is often modeled as a random process. In wireless systems, fading may either be due to multipath propagation, referred to as multipath-induced fading, weather, or shadowing from obstacles affecting the wave propagation, sometimes referred to as shadow fading."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/330px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fading","wikidata":"Q1196572"},{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog","wikidata":"Q37477"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/500px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/500px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frequency-selective time-varying fading causes a cloudy pattern to appear on a spectrogram. Time is shown on the horizontal axis, frequency on the vertical axis and signal strength as grey-scale intensity.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadio_propagation_noise.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Coherence_time.png/500px-Coherence_time.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Coherence_time.png/500px-Coherence_time.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoherence_time.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/500px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/1280px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/500px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=t